  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warming low-level thicknesses, possibly aided \nby a downsloping west to northwest low-level flow, should \nsupport above normal temperatures on Thursday. The one caveat is\nhigh clouds which may prevent highs from getting as warm as \nlow-level thickness values might otherwise suggest. \n\nBeyond Thursday, the pattern begins to change as an upper level\nridge shifts east from the TN Valley to the Carolinas. \nMeanwhile, a cutoff low is forecast to develop over east Texas. \nThe flow between these two features should help to steer TC \nIsaias north through the Gulf and towards the Gulf Coast. The \nlatest forecast track from the NHC brings Isaias north through \nthe TN and OH Valleys Saturday and Sunday after making landfall \nnear the FL/AL coast. East of Isaias, a plume of tropical \nmoisture, with anomalous 2\"+ PWATs, is forecast to surge north \nthrough the Southeast U.S. this weekend. The moisture in tandem \nwith enhanced low-level convergence and strengthening WAA along \na developing warm front should support a period of moderate to \nheavy rain late Saturday into Sunday across all of ENC. Guidance\ncontinue to show a solid signal for 1-2.5\" of rain across much \nof the area. While the area average amounts haven't changed \nmuch, the higher end of available guidance has increased some. \nThose higher end amounts (2-4\") will likely be focused where \nconvection develops. Initially, this will likely be focused \nalong the coast, and for now this is where the greatest \npotential exists for 2\"+ amounts. The precipitation is expected \nto trend more showery in nature later Sunday into Monday as the \nbetter forcing shifts away from the area. 6hr Flash flood \nguidance is around 2-4\" for the areas of ENC that saw heavy \nrainfall a few days ago, while the rest of the area is 4\"+. With\narea averages forecast to fall below these ranges, and over a \nlonger period of time, the risk of significant hydro impacts \nappears low. That said, any convection that develops will be \ncapable of intense rainfall rates, with rate-driven flooding of \npoor drainage and urban areas possible.\n\nOf note, based on the latest guidance, and NHC track, the \nremnants of Isaias may pass a bit closer than previously \nforecast. While this doesn't change the forecast significantly, \nit does edge the stronger gradient (between the TC remnants and \na strong high offshore) east some. Because of this shift, winds \nhave increased some with this forecast. Along the coast, a \nperiod of 30-40 mph wind gusts appears possible, especially \nSaturday night into Sunday night. \n\nOne other potential hazard we will be watching is \nthunderstorms. As mentioned previously, the greatest \ninstability, and risk of thunderstorms, is forecast to remain \noffshore. However, the inland track of the remnants of Isaias \nshould put ENC in the warm sector on Sunday, and recent guidance\nsuggests an area of weak to moderate instability may develop \nSunday afternoon depending on the track and timing, cloudcover, \netc. While low- level forcing is in question, it's a setup that \nbears watching for a conditional severe thunderstorm risk. \nMachine learning and EFI guidance have recently picked up on \nthis, and have trended higher with severe probs. \n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with a lower chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms by Tuesday.",
